  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 310 AM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nMoving into the extended forecast period, the forecast thinking has \nnot changed substantially in the last 24 hours.  The flow regime \nover the CONUS will feature an amplifying ridge over the western US \nwith downstream troughing across the eastern US.   This will result \nin a northwest flow regime across the Ohio Valley.  Numerous \nperturbations within the mean flow will drop southeast and into the \nbase of the trough during the week.  Each of these perturbations \nwill likely generate clusters of showers and thunderstorms.\n\nInitially, the storm track will be primarily north of the LMK CWA \nacross IL/IN/OH.  However, as the amplifying ridge grows out west, \nthe flow pattern will tilt more to the north-northwest by mid-late \nweek.  While the initial clusters of showers and storms will start \noff passing north of the region, we expect the storm track to shift \nsouthward with time and episodic bouts of convection will affect \nportions of southern IN and central and eastern KY.  Model soundings \nthrough the period continue show plentiful instability and a modest \nincrease in the shear profile. So, strong to severe storms will be \npossible with torrential rainfall and damaging winds being the main \nthreat.  There will be the potential for excessive rainfall as some \nareas will see repeated storms.  As of this writing, the best \nchances of excessive rainfall would likely be along and east of a \nline from Salem, IN southeast to Somerset, KY.\n\nThe highest chances of precipitation look to be in the Wednesday \nthrough Friday period.  Forecast uncertainty increases by late week \nas the models diverge a bit on the placement of a frontal boundary \nwhich will likely be draped across the region.  Depending on where \nthe boundary resides will determine where higher precipitation \nchances will be needed.  Overall, the pattern will be quite \nunsettled for the mid-late part of the week with near normal \ntemperatures and above normal rainfall.  Highs Tuesday and Wednesday \nwill be in the upper 80s to the lower 90s.  However, expect temps to \ncool off a bit with anticipated clouds/precipitation around in the \nThursday-Saturday period with highs mainly in the 80s.",
